<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2025-01-13.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="001637"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001637" label="[00:16:37]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Lance Vick&gt; In case is it is not on anyones radar yet, friend of mine took on our bounty for bootstrapping ada, so we can finally FSB build coreboot with stagex: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mgjm/annabella">https://github.com/mgjm/annabella</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="020611"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#020611" label="[02:06:11]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Lance Vick: nice</span></div><div class="line" id="221226"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221226" label="[22:12:26]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://mid-kid.root.sx/git/mid-kid/bootstrap/src/branch/master/gentoo-2025/gentoo.txt">https://mid-kid.root.sx/git/mid-kid/bootstrap/src/branch/master/gentoo-2025/gentoo.txt</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="221231"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221231" label="[22:12:31]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Updated my gentoo bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="221255"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221255" label="[22:12:55]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Not as huge a rewrite as a last time, but redid a few things.</span></div><div class="line" id="221337"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221337" label="[22:13:37]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Now relying more on portage's dependency resolution to figure things out, slightly less hardcoding.</span></div><div class="line" id="221407"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221407" label="[22:14:07]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">And the post-cross compilation steps are shorter too.</span></div><div class="line" id="221420"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221420" label="[22:14:20]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Only thing I didn't manage is upgrading to gcc 14.</span></div><div class="line" id="221430"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#221430" label="[22:14:30]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That must be done after install.</span></div><div class="line" id="222929"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#222929" label="[22:29:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mid-kid: nice! much shorter than your 1st version</span></div><div class="line" id="223034"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#223034" label="[22:30:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mid-kid: by the way, why are we unpacking squashfs tree rather than tar.xz?</span></div><div class="line" id="223311"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223311" label="[22:33:11]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Thanks! I had already shortened it significantly in 2024.8, I just hope this is more future-proof.</span></div><div class="line" id="223332"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#223332" label="[22:33:32]">&lt;mid-kid&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I use the squashfs tree because that one's kept in the mirrors forever, whereas the tar.xz tree vanishes in a month.</span></div><div class="line" id="223637"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#223637" label="[22:36:37]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh I see</span></div><div class="line" id="223950"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#223950" label="[22:39:50]">&lt;rkeene&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas, All my changes should be merged in if you want to check it out</span></div><div class="line" id="224121"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#224121" label="[22:41:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure, I'll update stage0-posix, though might not be today</span></div><div class="line" id="224347"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224347" label="[22:43:47]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">continued rebasing against linux-2.4; need a filesystem with 64bit timestamps with a 32bit posix linux-2.x abi</span></div><div class="line" id="224451"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#224451" label="[22:44:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">won't be easy, since internal data structures changed significantly between linux-2.6 and linux-2.4, nilfs2 was introduced with 2.6 series which i can't support yet with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="225546"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225546" label="[22:55:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">already pulled into some of the ancient grsecurity patches too, at least ASLR seems to work with x86_32 to shield against trouble</span></div><div class="line" id="225721"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225721" label="[22:57:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nilfs2 backporting is giving me a headache, needs a careful review, if and which filesystem could be prepared for 64bit timestamps</span></div><div class="line" id="225824"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225824" label="[22:58:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">too tried a few of various recent linux-live.iso which ship with some efi-compat boot loader and a compliant MBR with active partition marked</span></div><div class="line" id="225857"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#225857" label="[22:58:57]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">interestingly, the ancient thinkpad t40 PC-BIOS doesn't appreciate such hybrid iso when those contain an efi-compat boot-loader</span></div><div class="line" id="230030"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#230030" label="[23:00:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">seems intel wrecked UEFI/CSM comaptibility from both sides, uefi firmware itself and however the legacy compat boot-loaders are implemented nowadays, doesn't work reliably with an IBM PC BIOS</span></div><div class="line" id="230232"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#230232" label="[23:02:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with usb-sticks in particular</span></div><div class="line" id="232806"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#232806" label="[23:28:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">seems nilfs2 won't work with linux-2.4; giving up on this one radix-tree.h</span></div><div class="line" id="232920"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#232920" label="[23:29:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with nilfs2 support it's two criteria, it's specific features themselves, and 64bit timestamps</span></div><div class="line" id="233032"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233032" label="[23:30:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because, if i hacked ext2 for 64bit time, then this probably causes breakage in userspace with 32bit posix, while nilfs would not</span></div><div class="line" id="233212"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233212" label="[23:32:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's a little time remaining until hitting year2038 issues</span></div><div class="line" id="233252"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233252" label="[23:32:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i have another suspicion, why intel seems in a hurry to phase out x86/32, including pc-bios boot</span></div><div class="line" id="233317"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#233317" label="[23:33:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since whatever is hiding inside IntelME, if it's a 32bit posix i am not aware of any filesystem for year2038</span></div><br /></div></body></html>